WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:05.990
To the extent that there's a positive

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:08.100
to take out of this whole pandemic,

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.580
it's obviously been the heroic work

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.130
of the medical professionals.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:14.460
The doctors, the nurses,
the people that work

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.930
at the hospital on a day-to-day basis.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.740
So, today, I thought we would
focus on one of these stories

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.010
and head to Houston, where I grew up,

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:25.540
to talk with an old elementary
school of mine, Brent Kaziny.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:26.860
He's a doctor there.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.180
He works at the Texas Children's Hospital.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.280
And along with people on his team,

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:32.680
and other people at the hospital,

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.600
they came up with an open source design

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.920
for a protective system for people

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.987
who are performing intubations,

00:00:39.987 --> 00:00:43.030
to make sure the virus
wasn't spreading in the air.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:45.980
And, so with that, let's go talk to Brent

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.343
and hear his story.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:00.900
Besides being an illustrious alum

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.050
of Memorial Drive Elementary,

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:04.820
do you mind introducing yourself

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:07.340
and telling us kinda
what you do over there?

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.000
Yeah, sure, so, I'm the Medical Director

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.960
of Emergency Management at
Texas Children's Hospital.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.790
And what that really means is that just

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.040
when there are disaster
things that happen,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.100
hurricane, flooding, what have you,

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.860
I kind of help the
organization respond to that.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.860
Yeah, but obviously this is
quite a different situation.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.242
Whole different ball game.

00:01:28.242 --> 00:01:31.870
We are super prepared for hurricanes here.

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:33.995
We do annual drills on hurricanes

00:01:33.995 --> 00:01:37.000
that can obviously happen annually here.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.930
But I don't think any of us really

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.010
started laying the groundwork

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.810
for a response of this magnitude.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.290
I mean, it's just kind of nuts

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:45.830
with what we're going through right now

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:47.570
across the country and the world.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:48.820
Some of the early reports were like,

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:50.815
it doesn't seem to
affect children as much.

00:01:50.815 --> 00:01:52.800
I mean, what was your experience like?

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.010
I think the good news
through this whole thing is

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:59.351
that kids don't seem to be as
markedly impacted as adults.

00:01:59.351 --> 00:02:01.230
But we're now seeing these case reports,

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:03.426
this inflammatory disease process,

00:02:03.426 --> 00:02:07.220
inflammatory system response
that some kids are having

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.813
and there are reports out of
New York and UK about that.

00:02:09.813 --> 00:02:12.240
And so, we're obviously kind
of gearing up towards that

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.310
and making sure we're not
gonna miss any cases of those,

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.650
or kids that might progress to that.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.760
And so, just kinda going back,

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.360
you started seeing some pictures

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.377
coming out of intubating people.

00:02:23.377 --> 00:02:24.840
And I mean, tell me the story.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.010
I think you saw these pictures

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:27.910
and it kinda concerned you that maybe--

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:30.490
Yeah, I mean, really one of the things

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:32.740
that we were realizing
is that our providers

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.650
that intubate regularly,

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.710
so we're putting airways in and things,

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.910
they're at high risk of exposure, right?

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:42.070
There are secretions, you're
suctioning all this stuff.

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:43.270
And people were starting

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:46.420
to get really freaked
out about their potential

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.860
for contracting this
illness from patients.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.940
And so, I'd just kind of
been looking on the internet

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.120
and somebody sent me something.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.030
It was actually a physician in Taiwan,

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:59.490
had made this plexiglass rectangular box

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:00.957
with kinda two arm holes,

00:03:00.957 --> 00:03:02.830
and I was like, man, it's
a really interesting idea

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:06.460
but, I don't know that
ergonomically this would make sense

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.660
for the way that I would
put my arms and hands

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.990
and I don't know if the
access is limiting and--

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:13.180
So, you see in these pictures.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:15.340
You're like, I don't think
this is gonna work for me.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:16.330
Let's try something else.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:17.163
And so, what?

00:03:17.163 --> 00:03:19.160
Did you just literally start
sketching something out

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:20.441
on a piece of paper, or--

00:03:20.441 --> 00:03:24.150
So, it started with some
paper and some sketches.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.610
Then I literally went to
our office supply closet

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:31.300
and took all the paper
reams out of a cardboard box

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:32.430
and started hacking it up

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:33.982
and taping it together with duct tape.

00:03:33.982 --> 00:03:36.020
I mean, our facilities team is amazing

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:39.580
and in five days we
went from cardboard box

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:41.950
to functional working prototype.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:43.790
And so, out of this came

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:46.150
the Airway/Respiratory Containment System,

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.160
or the ARC as we call it.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.230
Yeah, so, walk me
through just a little bit

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:52.450
of the specifics of how it works.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.810
So, you essentially put the device

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:56.069
over the patient's head.

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:59.520
There's support holes for
personnel to get in there.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:00.610
And there's two other holes

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:02.060
that I think are really important too.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:04.950
And those holes we use to
create negative pressure.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:07.960
So, we hook a viral filter
up to one of those holes

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:11.380
and then some tubing that you
hook up to the wall suction.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.550
And so, it's essentially sucking...

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:15.850
Anything that's aerosolized
should get sucked out of there,

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:16.930
so it's not gonna come at you

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:19.070
when you take the device off, right?

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:20.860
So, put it on, I get in there.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:24.030
I put the airway in, hook
it up to the ventilator,

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:27.570
take the box off and it's
ready for the next patient.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.510
We're not trying to make money off of it.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:31.280
There's no IP.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:32.720
It's an open source project

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:34.820
that we're just sharing with the world.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.530
You guys open sourced so basically

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:39.110
that means you just
put up the CAD drawings

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:40.200
or the specs--
Yeah.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.840
And everything behind it?

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:43.420
There's a company called,

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:44.590
I think it's called Sawbones, right?

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:46.350
That's actually, they're making it?

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:48.830
Yeah, so they tweaked our final design.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:50.980
They kind of made a professional CAD of it

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.160
and now they have it on their website.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.170
If you want to download the sketch

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:56.150
and make it yourself in your wood shop,

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:57.370
just like we did, you can

00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:01.260
or you can buy one from
them and it's 130 bucks.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:03.637
And this is the device you guys are using

00:05:03.637 --> 00:05:06.520
in the hospital on kind
of day-to-day basis?

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.700
Yeah, so, we've got four
and they're being used

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.200
in the operating rooms
across the institution now.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.390
Have other hospitals heard about this yet?

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:16.900
Have they been able to order some as well?

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:18.910
It's all happening so quickly.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:20.640
I think there's interest.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.190
You know, I think in healthcare

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:23.910
and in particular
hospital based healthcare,

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:25.550
we tend to be lagging when it comes

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:27.690
to innovation and technology.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:31.610
And my hope is that in
the in the post COVID era,

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:33.050
we'll be able to use things like this

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:34.280
as an example and say,

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:36.780
look guys, we pushed this
through and got it done.

